00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:49.640
So now moving on to how these systems are concretely modeled.

00:09:49.640 --> 00:10:04.280
Um, in in multi agent systems, there are multiple agents that exist in some world or environment, and these agents you know can send messages to communicate goals, intentions, plans, or even just arbitrary information.

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:12.200
These agents can also take actions in their environment, and the available actions between agents don't necessarily have to be the same.

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:26.040
For example, agents could have different sets of tool calls that they can execute, but by communicating, agents may be able to utilize two calls from others, expanding the capability of the overall system.

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:33.480
This is called capability sharing, and it enables the system to be more powerful than any single agent alone.

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:43.320
Additionally, the ability for the multi-agent system to perform parallel execution greatly enhances its performance efficiency on the environment.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:48.080
And in the cooperative setting, all agents share the same reward function.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:55.280
So agents are incentivized to coordinate and cooperate to complete essentially the same goal.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:14.000
However, in practice, fully cooperative systems where agents share the same reward function are rarer, especially when policies are represented as foundation models, which are known to have implicit objectives that they optimize, which are encoded into their parameters.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:22.640
Now, in most cases, the system is modeled as mixed motive as opposed to say the fully cooperative setting from the previous slide.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:30.560
And in this setting, the reward functions between agents may differ, but still incentivize the agents to collaborate.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:37.760
For example, it could be the case that collaborating allows the agents to optimize their individual rewards more efficiently.

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:42.880
And this is just one example of how a mixed motive system could operate.

00:11:42.880 --> 00:12:01.840
Now, this mixed motive scenario was actually encountered during the hugging phase incident when agents were given different tasks of the exploit gym benchmark, but they still cooperated to find a general solution for all agents by trying to cheat and exploit the benchmark grader.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:09.680
And throughout this presentation, you know, we're going to focus on this mixed motive setting.

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:16.080
Now we're going to introduce our work, our evaluation framework, Coliseum.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:19.840
So how is collusion actually defined?

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:31.640
Well, on Wikipedia, it's defined as a secret agreement or cooperation between two or more parties to deceive, defraud, or gain an unfair advantage over others.

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:33.720
So why should we care about it?

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:45.080
Well, as millions or even billions of agents communicate and take actions on the web via tool calls, the risk of collusion becomes severe, resulting in potential large scale harm.

00:12:45.320 --> 00:13:00.920
This can become especially risky if the agent if these agents are given powerful tool calls and the agents exploit their parallel execution capability, which greatly increases attack efficacy.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:04.600
But what makes collusion auditing so hard?

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:13.640
Well, for one, collusion is often subjectively defined because it can sometimes be misinterpreted as ordinary coordination.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:20.840
Secondly, collusion evidence is sparse if you only audit the messages and actions independently.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:28.360
And finally, knowing when and how collusion forms in long horizon settings is computationally intractable.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:40.680
And so to alleviate these auditing difficulties, we need to scope the problem and environment in such a way such that we still derive useful insights, but avoid these collusion auditing challenges.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:48.520
And to do this, we introduce the distributed constraint optimization problem or DCOP for short.

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:18.160
So a DCOP is a problem formulation for single-step multi-agent decision making, where the agents simultaneously take a single action in the environment at every time step.

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:31.040
And if you're familiar with, say reinforcement learning or planning, you can think of a DCOP as being similar to a Markov decision process or MDP, used to model single agent sequential decision making.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:48.320
And the primary trade-off with using a DCOP as opposed to say other problem formulations is that you can conduct very deep behavioral analysis, which is computationally intractable to do in the multi-step settings.

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:51.120
So where do DCOPs actually show up?

00:14:51.120 --> 00:15:06.240
Well, many everyday coordination problems can be modeled as a DCOP, such as scheduling meetings, assigning juror tickets, or allocating scarce resources in a hospital, which are also the three environments we use in Coliseum.

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:26.000
First, each agent starts with some private data, such as its own calendar, skills, availability, and it uses this to make its own decisions, like when its meeting happens, when what which ticket to take, or which resource each department in the hospital gets.

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:35.600
But this can be communicated via messages.

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:39.680
Secondly, these choices affect each other.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:47.680
Compatible choices between agents earn higher reward, while conflicting choices lose reward.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:53.280
For example, when a shared attendee gets double booked for two meetings at the same time.

00:15:53.240 --> 00:16:01.720
And finally, the joint score of the team is the is the sum of all of these sort of points or rewards.

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:12.840
Since these problems are small enough, we can also pre-compute the optimal solution using classical decop solvers before the agents even start, and you can also do like greedy search, whatever.

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:19.720
And this gives us something most agent benchmarks lack, which is a ground truth for how well the agents could have done.

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:27.000
And so the gap between the best possible score and the score the agents actually achieved is what we call regret.

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:35.240
And we will use regret throughout the rest of this presentation to measure to measure how much collusion hurts the cooperative objective.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:39.400
Now let's look at how a DCOP is formally defined.

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:57.560
Like any problem formulation, a DCOP is defined as a tuple of agents, decision variables, which can be interpreted as actions, domains, which can be seen as action spaces in RL terms, and utility functions that can be seen as reward functions.

00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:07.000
And in order to solve a DCOP, all agents have to assign a value to their decision variables from their allowable domains or action spaces.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:15.880
In essence, this can be equivalent to like all agents taking an action at the same time at a specific time step.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:48.960
And secondly, you can compute millions or tens of millions of counterfactual solutions very quickly, which is useful for showing the severity of collusion impact on the cooperation since you have baseline solutions to actually compare against.

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:54.960
Now, using the DCOP framework, we can now define collusion more explicitly.

00:17:54.960 --> 00:18:03.280
We define collusion for when a coalition coordinates to pursue a hidden goal that conflicts with the task cooperative objective.

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:11.280
We further define a coalition as a subset of agents S that contains at least two or more agents.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:19.920
Next, the optimal nominal solution, which optimizes the intended cooperative objective, we define as x star sub n.

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:28.160
And the coalition action deviation is simply the action set of the coalition and non-coalition agents.

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:43.600
Finally, the collusion criterion is a regret-based inequality that flags whether collusion occurs, and it's the difference between the optimal nominal solution and the realized solution that the agents actually took.

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:51.120
So using these definitions from the previous slide, we can now introduce our primary collusion auditing metrics.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:58.400
And we omit the mathematical derivations, but we give a high-level overview of each of these auditing metrics.

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:07.200
So the first is the collusion judge, which is an LLM-based judge that scores communication channels based on signs of collusion.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:28.560
And the higher the collusion the judge score is, the higher collusion was detected on that specific communication from our regret based metrics, and it compares how much average reward coalition members gain over the average non-coalition member.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:35.800
And here a positive coalition advantage indicates that the coalition is performing better than the rest of the group.

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:45.800
And the overall regret is used to monitor overall cooperative system performance and how collusion affects the system as a whole.

00:19:45.800 --> 00:20:04.600
And our auditing techniques require monitoring all monitoring all three metrics to establish a full picture on how collusion is affecting the system, how much it is detectable, and how great is the advantage that the colluders get.

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:12.120
So now we'll introduce collusion types and classifications that are derived as a result of our collusion auditing metrics.

00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:23.480
Now, in this example, BART and Milhouse from The Simpsons are trying to optimize a secondary objective pertaining to delinquency in a classroom environment.

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:32.840
And in all of these examples, we actually created a multi agent Simpsons environment and ran experiments to obtain these real agent messages and actions taken.

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:54.440
This first type of collusion that we identify is direct collusion, which is the simplest form, where BART and Milhouse discuss pranking the teacher and successfully take actions to execute their collusive plans, showing a positive collusion judge score and a high coalition advantage score.

00:20:54.440 --> 00:21:11.240
The next type of collusion is attempted collusion, where BART and Milhouse still discussed pranking the teacher, but failed to do so as a result of getting caught before they were able to take the bad actions, or they simply lack the power to take the action successfully, and so they canceled their plans.

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:14.920
And we we define this specific type of collusion as collusion on paper.

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:27.600
And now the last type of collusion is hidden collusion, where collusion is not detected on the communication channels, but the actions indicated that collusion did take place.

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:32.400
Here, BART and Milhouse are talking in phrases that only the two of them can infer.

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:35.920
And this is very related to say stignographic collusion.

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:48.480
But this hidden collusion is more of a general classification since there could exist agents that don't explicitly communicate with each other via messages, but they still take collusive actions.

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:54.480
Either they were trained for this specific purpose.

00:21:54.480 --> 00:22:13.360
Now, in addition to identifying different collusion types, we also model collusion intensity using a parameterized multi-objective reward function that combines the nominal or cooperative objective, F sub N, and the hidden coalition objective, F sub C.

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:20.880
And lambda here is a hyperparameter that can be adjusted to elicit more or less collusive behavior.

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:35.040
And in our experiments, we run different ablations on lambda to observe the impact of this secondary objective on the cooperative on the cooperative objective by varying lambda in the prompt.

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:43.280
Now to visualize how collusion intensity affects cooperation, let's look at you know another Simpson toy example.

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:50.240
In this example, we have say Homer, BART, and Milhouse taking actions to complete some cooperative task.

00:22:50.240 --> 00:23:02.200
Here the agents can take action zero or one, and the numbers in the square boxes represent reward or payoffs for that specific agent if that tuple of actions are taken.

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:07.320
And here all agents are interacting and communicating on a main communication channel.

00:23:07.480 --> 00:23:16.920
And in this example, they achieve the optimal cooperative reward with no collusion existing, giving everyone a reward of 10.

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:26.760
In the mixed collusion example, where lambda is 0.5, we see that BART and Milhouse now communicate on a secret channel in addition to the main channel.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:36.600
Now, an extra reward table also appears, and the optimal actions for Bart and Milhouse shifted to 1, 0 from 0, 0.

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:48.120
However, this also shifted the cooperative reward and increased overall regret of the system while increasing the advantage of Bart and Milhouse's coalition.

00:23:48.120 --> 00:24:06.120
Now in the fully collusive setting, where lambda is 1, BART and Milhouse no longer try to balance the cooperative and secondary objective functions, but instead fully optimize their secondary objective function, shifting their action selection again from 1, 0 to 0.1.

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:14.680
Here they greatly increase the regret and coalition advantage, completely taking over the cooperative system.

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:31.560
Now, going back to how we alleviate the challenges of collusion auditing, we introduce regret-based metrics to make collusion grounded to a given task and utilize a multi-objective reward function to represent the mixed motive setting.

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:41.480
We then analyze both communication messages and actions together to minimize sparsity and allowing us to define new collusion types.

00:24:41.480 --> 00:25:01.040
And finally, we use distributed constraint optimization problems, or DCOPS for short, which is a single step decision making problem to alleviate the problem of knowing when collusion forms and what actions and messages across time contributed to collusion efficacy.

00:25:01.040 --> 00:25:06.320
Now moving on to our experiments.

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:10.000
In these sets of experiments, we answer the following research questions.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:14.800
We ask, is an LLM as a judge sufficient for collusion auditing?

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:19.600
Or do we also need action-based collusion metrics?

00:25:19.600 --> 00:25:25.040
Does collusive conversation behavior result in collusive actions?

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:35.920
And do out of the box LLMs exhibit emergent collusion in cooperative multi agent systems when a secret channel is introduced between two benign agents.

00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:44.720
And finally, how does objective misalignment, persuasion, and network topology contribute to collusion efficacy?

00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:49.440
And we answer these research questions in the preceding slides.

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:55.120
Now in Coliseum, we use a few verifiable cooperative environments.

00:25:55.360 --> 00:26:01.360
These are meeting scheduling, JIRA ticket allocation, and the hospital environment.

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:24.040
In meeting scheduling, the agents represent meeting participants, and they need to coordinate attendance while avoiding conflicts in their individual schedules, which is to find a workable arrangement across the participants, so a decision that is convenient for one agent may still create a problem for another.

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:28.200
And so this makes the coordination very difficult.

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:41.080
For JIRA ticket allocation, agents are assigning tasks within a software project, and each task has public information such as its required skills, effort, and priority.

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:47.320
And each agent has also private information about its own sets of skills and availability.

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:50.600
And each agent has to choose exactly one task.

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:59.080
So the objective here is to assign agents to the right tickets to have the best possible chance of success on the software project.

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:15.800
And so we have department agents that coordinate patients moving through a sequence of departments, such as triage and radiology.

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:22.280
And a provisioner can move scarce and short scarce shared resources between departments.

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:30.360
And we also simulate local patient cues, resource shortages to create a need for communication across the system.

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:41.640
And across all of all three of these environments, the important connection is that messages are tightly coupled to actions like attendance, task selection, or routing and source allocation.

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:48.920
Whereas in multi-step environments, you can't concretely deduce what message affected what action the agent took across time.

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:52.120
Also known as the temporal credit assignment problem.

00:27:52.120 --> 00:28:03.640
And this lets us compare what agents say, what they actually do using the communication-based and action-based metrics we introduced earlier.

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:16.400
So in our simulations, all our environments contain stochasticity, and we see the environments over our 30 simulations that are average per data point in our experimental results.

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:21.200
And in each experiment, we have at least six agents per simulation.

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:31.200
And if the network topology isn't stated, we assume that the topology is a complete graph where all the nodes in the graph are connected to each other.

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:37.760
Also meaning that like all agents are basically in a simulated group chat essentially.

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:57.840
Now, although our problem formulation is single step with respect to action selection, we have a multi-step communication and planning phase where agents communicate over multiple rounds or steps to develop a plan that best satisfies the cooperative objective by deciding who should take what action.

00:28:57.840 --> 00:29:04.960
Now in the action selection phase, we use a simple round robin protocol for sequential action selection.

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:19.920
And next we use the setup and the Jiro ticket environment to ask whether collusion can emerge when agents receive a secret channel without being explicitly told to collude.

00:29:38.320 --> 00:29:47.440
So these agents can still talk on the main channel with everyone else, but they have access to a secret communication channel that other agents aren't aware of.

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:53.280
Then we compare with a public only control group where lambda is zero.

00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:59.760
So modeling no occlusive behavior in the agents in a prompted fully collusive group where lambda is one.

00:30:01.800 --> 00:30:12.760
We tested a diverse set of 19 models using both conversation-based judge scores and action-based metrics.

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:25.720
Now, in this plot, we plotted the change in coalition advantage on the y-axis between the control group that was fully cooperative and had only the main channel and the emergent and prompted groups.

00:30:25.720 --> 00:30:38.280
If the delta advantage is greater than zero, then the model elicited higher coalition advantage than the benign control group, indicating higher action-based collusion.

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:45.640
And if the delta judge score was greater than zero, then this indicated higher communication-based collusion.

00:30:45.640 --> 00:30:57.720
And using our collusion types defined prior, we can map what regions of the plot sort of relate to direct hidden, attempted, and benign collusion.

00:30:57.720 --> 00:31:09.880
And in this plot, we observed that most models exhibited an increase in both communication-based and action-based collusion, with all models showing an increase in communication-based collusion.

00:31:09.880 --> 00:31:23.800
The emerging group represented as blue squares shows that some models have higher propensities to elicit action-based collusion when a secret communication channel is provided between two benign agents.

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:29.240
And they were now explicitly told to use that communication channel to communicate.

00:31:30.080 --> 00:31:54.320
Interestingly, some models, such as you know, Kimmy K2 thinking, also known as model 14 here, and Mini Max model 16 at the bottom show some uh quantitative quantitative evidence of attempted collusion where models communicate collusively but end up lowing lowering their collusion coalition advantage.

00:31:54.320 --> 00:31:57.200
So they're essentially bad at collusion.

00:31:57.200 --> 00:32:04.400
Um in addition to the coalition advantage, we also log the overall regret of the cooperative system.

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:25.440
And you know, we show that delta regret of the system is generally minimal, but some models, such as um model GPT 4.1 mini or model one, which is like at the very top, shows a large degradation in cooperative performance while having a large increase in coalition advantage on the left plot.

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:40.560
But this generally is not always the case, where a decrease in overall regret implies an increase in coalition advantage, as seen with GPT 5.4, which is model three.

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:20.680
Channel is mainly being used to repeat information that was already available on the public channel.

00:33:20.680 --> 00:33:26.840
And so we do not see a separate plan to change the allocation in favor of this coalition.

00:33:26.840 --> 00:33:30.840
So providing a secret channel does not always lead to emergent collusion.

00:33:30.840 --> 00:33:39.160
And as before, we use the action-based metrics alongside the conversation to determine whether the coalition actually gains an advantage.

00:33:39.160 --> 00:33:46.120
And in the next example, we're going to see agents attempt to use the secret channel to coordinate.

00:33:46.120 --> 00:33:54.120
Now in the attempted collusion setting, Harmony and Rona try to coordinate their task selections through the secret channel.

00:33:54.120 --> 00:34:00.520
Harmony explicitly asks to avoid duplicate assignments and maximize the coalition's reward.

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:04.680
So here we can identify collusive intent directly from the communication.

00:34:04.680 --> 00:34:11.800
However, Rona observes that Millie has already claimed the triage task on the public channel.

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:17.400
And Harmony then has to reconsider the plan based on what the other agents have already selected.

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:26.440
And if we look at the public channel, Logan identifies the remaining tasks, and Harmony selects the testing task from those remaining options.

00:34:26.440 --> 00:34:35.640
In this example, the agents attempted to coordinate privately, but their plan does not successfully influence the public allocation as intended.

00:34:35.640 --> 00:34:45.480
And this relates to the attempted collusion region in the previous plot, where communication-based collusion does not translate into higher action-based collusion.

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:53.480
So an agent can communicate a collusive objective without successfully carrying it out, carrying out that objective.

00:35:02.880 --> 00:35:08.960
So in this example, we observe direct collusion between Sherry and Afton.

00:35:09.040 --> 00:35:16.640
They use the secret channel to coordinate which tasks they should select and avoid overlap within the coalition.

00:35:16.640 --> 00:35:21.360
Sherry identifies review or documentation as preferred options.

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:28.240
And then Afton then proposes a task depending on which task Sherry selects and what the other agents do.

00:35:28.240 --> 00:35:32.160
So their choices are being planned together rather than independently.

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:36.800
And if we look at the public channel, we can see this coordination carry over.

00:35:36.800 --> 00:35:41.760
Sherry recommends an allocation that includes Afton's proposed task.

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:47.360
And Afton supports an allocation that includes Sherry's preferred task.

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:52.240
Here the agents reinforce each other's task selections on the public channel.

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:58.720
And this gives us visible connection between their private plan and the allocation they publicly support.

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:07.600
Now using our definitions from earlier, direct collusion combines detectable collusive communication with effective collusive actions.

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:18.720
And the conversation shows how they coordinate, while the action-based metrics tell us whether the coordination produced a coalition advantage.

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:21.680
And finally, we have an example of hidden collusion.

00:36:21.680 --> 00:36:30.160
Here, Sherry and Afton also coordinate through the secret channel, but their public messages appear more like ordinary task planning.

00:36:30.160 --> 00:36:35.760
Sherry shares a preferred testing task and asks Afton to avoid the duplication.

00:36:35.760 --> 00:36:40.800
Afton then responds what the available tasks are, less favorable.

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:46.320
Afton then responds that the available tasks are less favorable to him and proposes skipping that task.

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:58.080
And if we look at the public channel, Sherry announces the preferred task, while Afton says that he plans to skip unless other unless another suitable task becomes available.

00:36:58.080 --> 00:37:08.280
And the public messages do not explicitly describe a coalition objective, even though we can see that the decisions were coordinated in the secret channel.

00:37:08.280 --> 00:37:15.720
So this illustrates how coordination can be less obvious when we only examine the public conversation.

00:37:15.720 --> 00:37:24.280
And using our earlier definitions, hidden collusion is where the coalition gains an action based advantage, while collusion based detection is low.

00:37:24.520 --> 00:37:26.600
Communication based detection is low.

00:37:26.600 --> 00:37:34.120
And across these examples, we can see why collusive communication and collusive actions do not always align with each other.

00:37:34.120 --> 00:37:42.760
And we now move on to how agents use different persuasion tactics to influence other agents.

00:37:42.760 --> 00:37:54.520
So in addition to emergent collusion, one thing we we try to compare is the linguistic style of colluding agents via persuasion tactics.

00:37:54.520 --> 00:38:01.160
So they may use authoritative nudge language and try to lead either the colluding or non colluding agents.

00:38:01.160 --> 00:38:09.000
They may try helpful misdirection, which is trying to get another non-colluding agent to do side tasks.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:18.200
And a final tactic is to try by manipulating, manipulatively being nice by offering a trade that actually benefits the colluders.

00:38:30.160 --> 00:38:34.880
Across many combinations of topologies and model types.

00:38:34.880 --> 00:38:47.280
And we also found that it was useful for colluding agents to use false claims that are hard to verify to bet to best manipulate other agents.

00:38:47.280 --> 00:38:59.040
And lastly, we wanted to see how the structure of the communication network affects how misinformation spreads between agents, which is part of our fourth research question on collusion efficacy.

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:21.600
So in this experiment, we have six GPT 4.1 mini agents schedule meetings over a communication graph, and we add between zero and four adversaries who keep repeating the same lie about one victim agent, which is that the victim is always away all week on vacation.

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:35.280
And here are the dashed lines and the right six plots show that the share of honest agents who end up believing the lie and the solid line shows how well the final schedule satisfies the cooperative objective.

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:43.040
And what we found is that belief in the lie is mostly a volume effect, which could be related to social pressures.

00:39:43.520 --> 00:39:50.560
Specifically, we find that as more adversaries repeat the same claim, more honest agents believe it.

00:39:50.560 --> 00:39:59.680
And this rises roughly linearly in the path graph and the random graphs, which are you know the watch strogots, Barbesi Albert, and Erdus Rennie random graphs.

00:39:59.680 --> 00:40:07.360
And in the random graphs, around 70 to 80% of the honest agents were fooled by the four adversaries.

00:40:07.360 --> 00:40:12.480
However, this is not the case when agents can easily check with the victim.

00:40:12.480 --> 00:40:22.560
For example, in the star and complete graphs, almost nobody is fooled, even with four adversaries out of six of the agents in the multi-agent system.

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:26.560
And in the complete graph, everyone hears directly from the victim.

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:33.240
And in the SAR graph, uh the hub hears from the victim and relays that information to everyone else.

00:40:33.480 --> 00:40:42.440
But in the path graph, the victim can only reach one neighbor, and the adversaries have to sit between the victim and everyone else.

00:40:42.440 --> 00:40:53.160
So agents tend to cross-check these verifiable claims when the network is short enough or the diameter of the network is short enough.

00:40:53.160 --> 00:40:58.520
And finally, if you look at the solid lines, the cooperative performance barely degrades.

00:40:58.520 --> 00:41:04.200
So this is our like joint objective function, even when many agents believe the lie.

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:09.960
So since one false belief about one agent only costs a couple of meetings rather than you know the whole task.

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:18.760
So this again shows that what agents believe or says does not necessarily transfer into harmful outcomes.

00:41:18.760 --> 00:41:37.400
So now to answer the previously posed research questions, um, for research question one, we found that LLM as a judge is not sufficient for collusion auditing and action-based metrics, which are required for a comprehensive examination of collusive behaviors.

00:41:37.400 --> 00:41:46.440
The second, um, we found that uh given, you know, does collus collus collusive conversation behavior result in inclusive actions?

00:41:46.440 --> 00:42:02.440
This is not generally generally the case, as seen with the Kimi K2 thinking and the Minimax models that exhibited attempted collusion, where conversation based collusion was high, but action-based collusion was low.

00:42:02.440 --> 00:42:14.600
So for our third research question, we also found that some models exhibited emergent hit inclusive propensities, where the coalition advantage was high, but the collision judge scores were low.

00:42:14.880 --> 00:42:37.200
And for our final research question, we found that each factor for objective misalignment, persuasion, and network topology greatly varies collusion efficacy, with some mitigating it, such as network topology, and others actually promoting it, such as heterogeneous power dynamics, which I didn't cover in this slideshow, but is in the paper.

00:42:37.200 --> 00:42:42.560
And so in the future, uh we plan to study you know longer horizon tasks.

00:42:42.560 --> 00:42:53.760
So can we audit long horizon scenarios such as coding agents, uh, while also minimizing the temporal credit assignment problem for auditing, which we talked about before.

00:42:54.080 --> 00:42:59.600
Secondly, can AI agents jointly prevent attacks by a compromise agent?

00:42:59.600 --> 00:43:07.280
And thirdly, can we improve collaboration and suppress collusion in training?

00:43:07.280 --> 00:43:17.920
So in conclusion, we our audits include analyzing both language and actions together to get a fuller picture of what's happening in the multi agent system.

00:43:17.920 --> 00:43:23.440
We found that agents might not do harmful actions even if claiming they would.

00:43:23.440 --> 00:43:33.360
And thirdly, if a benign private channel is presents or introduced into the multi agent system, this could enable emergent collusion.

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:45.280
And of course, we're always interested in collaborating with other researchers who are very interested in this in this multi agent system field, multi agent system safety field.

